   "Advisory-only twin review (simulated consent never counts as real consent), grounded in ratified docs/VALUES.md (CID 2b6d6a6a794ef168a00184faf35d3b77efb083c24671e3ff1092a432405f9448) and individual agency to act and object. BIP-019's recovery intent is right, but as written it opens four serious loopholes: (1) Retroactive ratification & agency: section 3 formalizes 'incident response ships first, process ratifies after' for live consensus code (b52a95c) without a D39.6 bounded emergency-exception record, full 64-char :patch-cid and bundle CIDs reachable on /artifact/v1/blob/<cid>, or any rollback/remediation trigger if a member raises a paramount objection during the N=10,000-epoch window—turning consent over running state-repair code into a fait accompli and dropping extension-by-objection. (2) Duration soak: restricting staging-first soak to 'any follow-up code' exempts b52a95c itself and relies on a 4-minute/41-epoch P-MEM-2 burst while leaving 'declare a soak duration' unquantified and silent on fork, term-divergence, and >256-epoch pruning fault injection. (3) Generator realism: deferring the generator case to Stage 6 reverses strict TDD ('no fix without its repro/generator first') and invites a static corrupted-depth fixture instead of a Malli/test.check stateful command-sequence generator over record-block-depth (direct and ancestor-loop branches), pruning, and repair-corrupt-block-depths convergence. (4) Honest error-budget & rollback-first accounting: a ~20-minute SEV-1 consensus deadlock consumes ~38x a six-nines annual finality budget; Postmortem #2 must pair the 3-way SLO split and rollback-first decision forensics (why LKG rollback was bypassed or could not clear the latent deadlock) with explicit error-budget freeze discipline.",
