   :critique
   "BIP-019's recovery intent aligns with our safety aims, but under individual agency and Values CID 2b6d6a6a794ef168a00184faf35d3b77efb083c24671e3ff1092a432405f9448 I withhold clean consent until three soak, VOPR, and gating loopholes are closed. First, citing P-MEM-2 Run 2 (a 4-min, 41-epoch :443 memory hammer, 85906->85947) as post-fix proof conflates emergency containment with an honest empirical soak gate, and Section 4.3 leaves 'duration soak for finality' completely unquantified: a 4-min run never even crosses one full default-streamlet-retention-epochs window (256 epochs, ~26 min). Second, deferring a static 'finalized parent cached depth > tip depth' fixture to Stage 6 is too weak for VOPR: in streamlet/core.cljc, BOTH the fast-path (contains? executed-roots p-hash) check and the ancestor-walk loop checked executed-roots before depths, and ingest-verified-ancestors also anchors unrooted blocks by :epoch rather than tip depth; we need deterministic dynamic fault injection plus a non-vacuity MUT-* mutant and a counted :diagnose effect whenever repair-corrupt-block-depths mutates state (Rule 5). Third, 'staging-first soak default applies to any follow-up code' lacks concrete staging exit criteria, pins only 7-char hashes (b52a95c/bec0a72) instead of 40/64-char CIDs, and omits exact 3-way SLO error-budget numbers (~1,200,000 ms finality stall vs 86.4 ms daily six-nines budget, i.e., ~13,889x burn).",
